WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.333
Supervisor and hierarchical patterns.

2
00:00:03.633 --> 00:00:07.833
Instead of hoping the model got it right, put a checkpoint in front of every answer.

3
00:00:09.033 --> 00:00:12.933
In the supervisor pattern, one agent is in charge of the others.

4
00:00:13.233 --> 00:00:21.567
It assigns work, reviews each output against explicit criteria, and demands a retry with specific critique when a check fails.

5
00:00:21.867 --> 00:00:24.367
You trade extra tokens for reliability.

6
00:00:25.567 --> 00:00:26.567
Here is the loop.

7
00:00:26.867 --> 00:00:28.400
The worker drafts an answer.

8
00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:31.367
The supervisor checks it against a concrete list.

9
00:00:31.667 --> 00:00:35.247
If a check fails, the worker gets exactly which one and why.

10
00:00:35.247 --> 00:00:38.828
That specificity makes the second try better than the first.

11
00:00:38.828 --> 00:00:40.200
And the loop is capped.

12
00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:42.967
Three rules make this work.

13
00:00:43.267 --> 00:00:44.519
Criteria, not vibes.

14
00:00:44.519 --> 00:00:48.900
The supervisor checks a concrete list, not whether the answer is good.

15
00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:51.733
Specific critique, naming the failed check.

16
00:00:52.033 --> 00:00:55.200
And bounded loops: past the cap, escalate to a human.

17
00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:57.800
Here is the loop in code.

18
00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:03.567
Up to three times, the worker drafts and the supervisor checks the draft against the criteria.

19
00:01:03.867 --> 00:01:05.197
If it passes, we stop.

20
00:01:05.197 --> 00:01:09.067
If not, the failures are added to the task for the next attempt.

21
00:01:09.367 --> 00:01:12.957
And if all three fail, the else branch escalates to a human.

22
00:01:12.957 --> 00:01:14.333
Never an infinite loop.

23
00:01:15.533 --> 00:01:16.900
Here is a real catch.

24
00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:25.400
The weekly report writer stated a CME speed of three thousand one hundred kilometers per second, a number that appears nowhere in the data.

25
00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:32.567
The third criterion, no numbers absent from tool outputs, fails, and the writer is told exactly why.

26
00:01:32.867 --> 00:01:35.115
The revision cites the real figure.

27
00:01:35.115 --> 00:01:40.767
The cost was two extra model calls, and no fabricated number ever reached a stakeholder.

28
00:01:41.967 --> 00:01:42.779
Let's recap.

29
00:01:42.779 --> 00:01:46.300
A supervisor reviews worker output against criteria.

30
00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:48.867
Failures come back as specific critique.

31
00:01:49.167 --> 00:01:51.400
Loops are capped, then a human steps in.

32
00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:54.299
You spend tokens to get a checkable gate.

33
00:01:54.299 --> 00:01:55.567
Next up: reflection.
